# 24. TCP ANALYSIS

TCP is connection-oriented.

The connection starts with the:

> **TCP 3-way handshake**

```text
Client                    Server
  │                         │
  │──── SYN ───────────────>│
  │<─── SYN, ACK ───────────│
  │──── ACK ───────────────>│
  │                         │
  │      Connection         │
```

---

# 25. TCP FLAGS

Important TCP flags:

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| SYN | Start/synchronize connection |
| ACK | Acknowledge |
| FIN | Graceful termination |
| RST | Reset connection |
| PSH | Push data |
| URG | Urgent pointer |

---

# 26. FILTER TCP SYN

Display packets with SYN set:

```text
tcp.flags.syn == 1
```

Only initial SYN packets, excluding SYN-ACK:

```text
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
```

This can help investigate connection attempts.

---

# 27. TCP SCANNING INDICATOR

Suppose one source sends many SYN packets:

```text
Attacker → Port 21
Attacker → Port 22
Attacker → Port 23
Attacker → Port 25
Attacker → Port 80
Attacker → Port 443
...
```

This may indicate:

> Port scanning or service discovery.

But:

> A pattern alone is not proof of malicious activity.

It needs context and validation.

---

# 28. TCP RESET

A TCP RST can indicate:

- Connection rejection
- Application behavior
- Closed service
- Unexpected termination
- Firewall behavior
- Network problems

Do not automatically label every RST as malicious.

---

