# 49. FTP

FTP is traditionally plaintext.

Common ports:

```text
TCP 21 → Control
TCP 20 → Traditional data channel
```

A packet capture can expose application data when FTP is used without encryption.

This demonstrates why secure alternatives are preferred.

---

# 50. TELNET

Telnet traditionally provides plaintext remote terminal communication.

Default port:

```text
TCP 23
```

A packet capture can potentially expose credentials and commands.

Secure alternative:

```text
SSH
```

Default SSH port:

```text
TCP 22
```

---

# 51. SSH

SSH provides encrypted remote administration.

Default:

```text
TCP 22
```

In a normal capture, you will see encrypted SSH traffic rather than readable commands/passwords.

